 \section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
-A software reviewer may defer a warning for a particular revision of a component. A later finding requires two decisions. Does the finding warrant an active review episode, a period in which the issue qualifies for review? If it does, does the earlier deferral still authorize withholding the warning? A monitor can answer the first question correctly and still hide a warning the reviewer should see. We identify which version comparisons preserve these decisions under a specified review policy, using proofs over event sequences and a controlled replay.
-
-In a constructed audit case, positive findings begin at one second and qualify the current revision for a warning at three seconds. At two seconds, the monitor receives a deferral that names a different evidence revision and expires at four seconds. A monitor that skips the deferral's version check hides the warning at three seconds. A monitor checking the authorized revision shows it. Both record the same active episode and show the warning at expiry. The difference is in what the reviewer sees at the qualifying event.
-
-Two established capabilities explain how this situation can arise. Nweke and Yeng tie compliance evidence to releases and record the policy under which it was evaluated \cite{nwekeyeng2026compliance}. Prometheus Alertmanager retains active alerts and can withhold their notifications under a matching temporary silence \cite{prometheus2026alertmanager}. Henzinger et al. formalize how histories assigned to the same monitor class receive one output, and Jevitha et al. construct abstractions of program executions for selected properties \cite{henzinger2022abstract,jevitha2024abstract}. For revision-sensitive warnings, the unresolved interface question concerns the required output: which distinctions among the current versions and the versions named by a finding or deferral must reach a monitor that displays the warning, and which must also reach a monitor that explains it?
-
-We investigate this question using five source checks from a system that links key performance indicator (KPI) documentation to source artifacts \cite{mouawad2026architecture}. Each check supplies a finding about one artifact. An \emph{event} records a review time, the current evidence and policy versions, and any finding and deferral supplied at that time. A \emph{trace} is an ordered sequence of such events for one artifact and rule family. A deferral is the authorization to withhold a warning; \emph{suppression} is its effect on the displayed decision. The monitor uses successive events to open, sustain, clear or escalate an episode and to determine the warning shown at each event.
-
-The evaluation follows three questions. \textbf{Q1:} Which version distinctions preserve exact decision sequences and their reasons? \textbf{Q2:} How does omitting evidence-version, deferral-version or stale-evidence checks affect the displayed warning and the underlying episode? \textbf{Q3:} Do irrelevant metadata edits or equivalent changes of time unit alter the output? We connect these questions through three contributions:
+A warning produced from source-code review may remain visible long after the code that triggered it has changed. A reviewer may also defer a warning for a particular revision while investigating it. When another revision arrives, the system must make two decisions: does the available finding justify an active review episode (a period during which the issue qualifies for review), and does the earlier deferral still authorize hiding the warning? These decisions need not agree. In the controlled study reported here, removing the deferral-version check changed 30 of 987 sequences of displayed decisions even though the sequences of active review episodes remained identical. This difference motivates the question of which version information a review monitor must retain.
+
+The setting arises when enterprises explain key performance indicators (KPIs) to people who do not maintain the code that calculates them. A KPI such as yield or throughput may be calculated in a database view or a program, while its business definition, formula and owner appear in separate documentation. LEAP (Literate Programming for Automated KPI Extraction) reconstructs source-linked KPI knowledge records from these artifacts and publishes their formulas, source locations, business context and review signals through an Interactive Bluebook, a browsable collection of linked KPI documentation pages \cite{mouawad2026architecture}. Its source-analysis checks can supply findings that invite further review. The present research begins at that point: it investigates what should happen to a source-review finding as the relevant evidence, governing policy and reviewer's deferral change over time.
+
+Consider one constructed sequence. A positive finding for a particular source revision begins at one second and remains present until the review policy's qualification period is satisfied at three seconds. A deferral received at two seconds names an older evidence revision and expires at four seconds. The underlying finding qualifies for review at three seconds regardless of the deferral. A monitor that checks the version named by the deferral displays an active warning; one that accepts the obsolete deferral displays a suppressed warning instead. At four seconds both display the warning because the deferral has expired. The episode itself is active in both implementations throughout the disputed interval. Thus counting active episodes cannot reveal the warning withheld from the reviewer.
+
+This problem connects two established lines of research. Evidence and compliance systems associate findings with releases and policy versions \cite{nwekeyeng2026compliance}, and notification systems such as Prometheus Alertmanager separate stored active alerts from temporarily silenced notifications \cite{prometheus2026alertmanager}. Runtime-monitoring research also establishes that a reduced input representation can preserve a selected output only when histories made indistinguishable by that reduction require the same output \cite{henzinger2022abstract,jevitha2024abstract}. These results explain the need for provenance, suppression rules and information-preserving abstractions. For a review monitor that both follows evidence revisions and reports why a warning is shown or withheld, a more specific question remains: which comparisons among current versions, observation versions and deferral versions are sufficient for each of its outputs?
+
+We answer that question by specifying an executable review lifecycle over ordered events. Each event identifies the relevant source-evidence revision, the applicable review-policy version, and any observation or deferral supplied at that time. An \emph{observation} reports the result of one source check; a \emph{deferral} is a version-scoped permission to withhold a displayed warning. A \emph{review episode} is the underlying period during which the policy considers a finding active. A \emph{trace} is the time-ordered sequence of events for one source artifact and rule family. The monitor evaluates each event, updates the episode and produces a displayed state, activity flag, severity label and reason. The mathematical question is whether upstream code can replace the raw version identifiers with comparison results without changing these outputs.
+
+Three questions guide the study. \textbf{Q1} asks which version distinctions suffice to preserve the complete event decision, and which also preserve the reason selected by the policy. \textbf{Q2} asks how removing version applicability or evidence-freshness checks changes displayed warnings and active episodes. \textbf{Q3} asks whether irrelevant display metadata and equivalent time units leave the monitor's outputs unchanged. The study makes three connected contributions:
