-\item An executable applicability contract separates the evidence supporting a review episode from the authorization to suppress its warning.
-\item Sufficiency proofs identify three joint comparisons for displayed state, activity and severity, and five comparisons that also preserve the specified reason. Constructed pairs expose the decision loss caused by deleting a version field without retaining its comparison.
-\item An evaluation of complete decisions and active episodes exposes an obsolete-deferral error: accepting such deferrals preserves all 987 held-out base episode sequences and changes 30 complete decision sequences.
+\item A fully specified, event-based review contract that checks whether source findings and review deferrals apply to the current evidence and policy before determining the displayed warning.
+\item Information-preservation proofs showing that three joint version comparisons suffice for the displayed decision, activity and severity, whereas five comparisons suffice when the monitor must also give the specified reason. Constructed paired traces demonstrate the consequences of omitting individual version fields without retaining their comparisons.
+\item A controlled evaluation that distinguishes an unchanged active episode from an incorrect displayed decision. In the held-out base traces, accepting obsolete deferrals left all 987 episode sequences unchanged and altered 30 complete decision sequences.
 \end{itemize}
