-Evidence age acts earlier in the decision. A stale positive observation can sustain a qualification run, whereas a stale negative observation can sustain a clearing run. Accepting the former can raise an unwarranted warning, and accepting the latter can clear an episode too early. These transitions explain why removing the stale-age check produced active-event errors in both directions. The metadata and time-unit checks address a separate question. Even implementations that accepted obsolete or stale inputs preserved both transformations, so an evaluation of semantic agreement also needs cases that exercise the disputed condition.
-
-The choice of interface follows from the monitor's outputs. A service with access to the current and supplied version names can send their comparisons with each event. A monitor producing decisions can combine evidence and policy into a single pair. A monitor reporting the specified reasons can use five comparisons: separate current-version change and observation-version match indicators together with a joint suppression match. The deferral uses a joint match and expiry because its reason identifies a suppression that actually affected the display. This makes concrete the dependence of a monitoring abstraction on the property being checked \cite{henzinger2022abstract,jevitha2024abstract}.
-
-The same policy ran in the incremental implementation, SQLite and an established runtime-monitoring engine. Agreement across those executions locates the design choice at the boundary between the producer of evidence and the monitor. Retaining version relationships upstream supplies the distinctions the policy uses, and scoring the complete displayed decision downstream exposes an obsolete authorization even when its underlying episode is correct.
+The main result concerns a failure of evaluation criteria as much as a failure of a monitor. An active-episode metric asks whether the underlying issue should currently qualify for review. It does not ask whether an authorized warning is actually shown. When the monitor accepts a deferral for an old evidence revision, it can retain the correct episode and still suppress the new revision's warning. In the constructed case \path{audit/suppression_old_context/2/base}, the obsolete deferral for \code{c0} hides the warning for \code{c1} at qualification; at expiry both implementations display the active warning. The 30 affected held-out base traces make this distinction observable. A review-system evaluation therefore needs a decision-level endpoint as well as an episode-level endpoint.
+
+A different loss of information occurs when the observation, rather than the deferral, is no longer current. Under the specified policy, a stale positive observation cannot legitimately continue qualification and a stale negative observation cannot legitimately continue clearing. Removing the freshness check lets both do so and produces unwarranted and missed active decisions. This mechanism explains the opposite directions of error in the no-freshness ablation. The invariance tests serve another purpose: they show that changing unrelated presentation metadata or expressing the same event times in milliseconds leaves the decision unchanged. A method can pass those invariance checks and still mishandle a version or freshness boundary, so the two forms of testing answer different questions.
+
+The formal result explains what an upstream evidence-producing system must communicate to a downstream monitor. Because the lifecycle uses version names only to determine changes and matches, the producer need not always send the names themselves. If the monitor reports displayed state, activity and severity, three joint comparisons preserve those decisions under the specified policy and retained timing and value inputs. If it must also report why a finding is unresolved or why an episode reset, five comparisons preserve the policy's reason distinctions. The reason output therefore changes what counts as a sufficient abstraction. This concretely instantiates the dependence of monitor abstractions on required outputs established in earlier work \cite{henzinger2022abstract,jevitha2024abstract,antignac2017minimisation}.
+
+The source-documentation setting gives those distinctions a practical interpretation. LEAP reconstructs KPI knowledge records so that business descriptions and review signals remain linked to source evidence \cite{mouawad2026architecture}. The lifecycle studied here would receive a rule finding tied to that source evidence, check whether it is current under the applicable policy, and then decide whether a matching deferral may hide the warning. The study's incremental, SQLite and PyContract implementations produced the same recorded decisions from that contract. Their agreement makes the proposed boundary between source analysis and monitoring inspectable: the producer establishes the relevant evidence identity and Boolean finding; the monitor evaluates time and applicability; the receiving interface chooses whether it requires a decision alone or a decision with its reason.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-With observation values, times, packet presence, expiry, policy parameters and rule severity retained, a version-sensitive review monitor can replace version identifiers with three comparisons: whether the current version pair changed, whether a supplied observation matches that pair, and whether a supplied deferral matches it. Five comparisons additionally preserve the contract's reason label: separate change and observation-match indicators identify the source of a reset or observation mismatch, while one joint comparison suffices for suppression. The 30 held-out base sequences on which an obsolete deferral changed the display despite an identical episode interval show why the acceptance test must compare the warning as well as its activity. A next evaluation can fix an organization's review and deferral rules, replay its historical events in order, and compare predicted intervals, displayed warnings and reason labels with the corresponding recorded decisions.
+A reviewer-facing warning and the underlying review episode are separate outputs of a version-sensitive monitoring policy. The controlled study shows why that separation matters: accepting obsolete deferrals changed 30 of 987 held-out base decision sequences while leaving all 987 active-episode sequences unchanged. The resulting error would be missed by an evaluation that counted only whether review episodes were active.
+
+For the specified policy, raw evidence and policy version identifiers can be replaced by three joint comparisons when the monitor outputs displayed state, activity and severity. Five comparisons preserve those outputs and the policy's selected reason, given the retained observation values, packet presence, event times, expiry, policy parameters and severity labels. The proofs establish these sufficiency results; the event-trace comparisons show their operation in the constructed benchmark. The next empirical question is how this contract behaves with an organization's recorded source revisions, deferrals and review actions, using observed decisions as the evaluation reference.
 
 \section{Limitations}
 The lifecycle policy and temporal traces are constructed research materials. The reset, timing and suppression rules require validation against an externally established review policy and its recorded decisions. All five source-rule families share the same 329 temporal templates, so the held-out partition measures conformance across family instances. Generalization to unseen temporal patterns and deployed systems remains unmeasured. The source predicates are lexical review signals; their finding accuracy and the interpretation of their rule names are upstream of the evaluated temporal decision. Relevant-evidence equivalence is also supplied as an input and requires an upstream definition of which source changes preserve it.
